      "provenance": {
        "notes": "Seasonal pot plant: held until spring and re-flowered only with long dark nights (NC State, RHS and Clemson all describe the short-day routine); no rest-season number. Toxicity sources disagree on how bad it is: ASPCA lists it as toxic to dogs and cats (irritant sap, mouth and stomach irritation, sometimes vomiting) while saying the danger is overrated; NC State rates humans low; Clemson says not considered toxic; RHS says much less toxic than other euphorbias and warns about sap on skin and eyes; the Penn State holiday-plants page was truncated and only a summary of it was read (says exaggerated). Pets graded 1 (the agent's mapping, higher reading kept). Sap in the eye: NC State says temporary to permanent corneal damage, graded 2 by the agent. Temperature merges RHS minimum 13 to 16 C with NC State 65 to 70 F and Clemson 'not above 70 F'. Size: RHS 60 by 30 cm (shop plants are growth-suppressed; NC State says wild plants reach 10 ft). Humidity: RHS says humidity extends flowering, no figure. Growth rate: none states it."
      },
